  "full_response": "Kubernetes is a powerful orchestrator for containerized applications, and it has sophisticated mechanisms for pod scheduling and resource management. Here's an overview of how it handles these tasks:\n\n### Pod Scheduling\n\n1. **Scheduler Daemon**: Kubernetes has a component called the kube-scheduler that is responsible for scheduling pods onto nodes in the cluster. The scheduler's job is to place new pods onto nodes with sufficient resources while respecting all constraints and policies.\n\n2. **Pod Affinity and Anti-affinity**: These are rules that you can specify to influence the scheduler's decision. Pod affinity allows you to co-locate pods on nodes based on constraints, while anti-affinity ensures that certain pods are not placed on the same node.\n\n3. **Node Affinity/Anti-affinity**: Similar to pod affinity, but these constraints are defined on the node level. It allows you to influence where pods should be placed based on node labels.\n\n4. **Taints and Tolerations**: Nodes can be tainted to repel pods unless those pods have a toleration for the taint. This is useful for dedicating certain nodes for specific purposes, such as running only GPU workloads.\n\n5. **Resource Requirements and Limits**: When you specify resource requests and limits for a pod, the scheduler ensures that the node it is being placed on has the required resources available. Requests specify the minimum guarantees, and limits specify the maximum an application is allowed to use.\n\n6. **Priority and Preemption**: Kubernetes allows setting priority levels for pods, which can cause lower-priority pods to be evicted if a higher-priority pod needs placement on the same node.\n\n7. **Custom Scheduling**: You can extend scheduling policies with custom schedulers if the default one does not meet your needs.\n\n### Resource Management\n\n1. **Resource Quotas**: These allow you to define constraints on the resources that a namespace can consume, such as CPU, memory, and storage.\n\n2. **Node Resource Limits**: Each node in the cluster has limits defined by its hardware capabilities and any resource configurations set by the node's taints or labels.\n\n3. **Quality of Service Classes**: Kubernetes categorizes pods into different QoS classes (Guaranteed, Burstable, and BestEffort) based on their resource requests and limits to manage resource utilization more predictably.\n\n4. **Vertical and Horizontal Pod Autoscaling**: \n   - **Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA)** automatically adjusts the CPU and memory resources requested by pods based on usage.\n   - **Horizontal Pod",
